window.QB_SUB = {"code":"B.3","title":"Gas laws","source":"d76e27b2-B3_Gas_laws_questions.json","p1a":[{"id":"19M.1A.SL.TZ1.13","key":"qcm_a28992795606","tag":"","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":13,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>A gas storage tank of fixed volume <em>V</em> contains <em>N</em> molecules of an ideal gas at temperature <em>T</em>. The pressure at kelvin temperature <em>T</em> is 20 MPa.&nbsp;<span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"\\frac{N}{4}\"> <mfrac> <mi>N</mi> <mn>4</mn> </mfrac> </math></span></span> molecules are removed and the temperature changed to 2<em>T</em>. What is the new pressure of the gas?</p><p>A. 10 MPa</p><p>B. 15 MPa</p><p>C. 30 MPa</p><p>D. 40 MPa</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"19M.1A.SL.TZ2.12","key":"qcm_f4036d4a0136","tag":"","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">A container holds 20 g of argon-40(<span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"{}_{18}^{40}{\\text{Ar}}\"> <msubsup> <mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>18</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>40</mn> </mrow> </msubsup> <mrow> <mtext>Ar</mtext> </mrow> </math></span>)&nbsp; and 40 g of neon-20 (<span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"{}_{10}^{20}{\\text{Ne}}\"> <msubsup> <mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>20</mn> </mrow> </msubsup> <mrow> <mtext>Ne</mtext> </mrow> </math></span>) .<br></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">What is<span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"\\frac{{{\\text{number of atoms of argon -40}}}}{{{\\text{number of atoms of neon -20}}}}\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>number of atoms of argon -40</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>number of atoms of neon -20</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span> in the container?<br></span></p><p>A. 0.25<br></span></p><p>B. 0.5<br></span></p><p>C. 2<br></span></p><p>D. 4</span></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"19M.1A.SL.TZ2.13","key":"qcm_07adb88678b9","tag":"","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":13,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">The equation <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"\\frac{pV}{T}\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mi>V</mi> </mrow> <mi>T</mi> </mfrac> </math></span> </span><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">= constant is applied to a real gas where <em>p</em> is the pressure of the gas, <em>V</em> is its volume and <em>T</em> is its temperature.<br></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">What is correct about this equation?<br></span></p><p>A. It is empirical.<br></span></p><p>B. It is theoretical.<br></span></p><p>C. It cannot be tested.<br></span></p><p>D. It cannot be disproved.</span></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>A</p> <p>This is a Nature of Science question and was poorly answered by candidates. It had a very low discrimination index and 60% of candidates chose response B when A was the accepted answer. The question is in the context of a real gas. For publication this will be made clearer.</p>"},{"id":"19M.1A.SL.TZ2.14","key":"qcm_f25f964388b5","tag":"","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":14,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Cylinder X has a volume <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"V\"> <mi>V</mi> </math></span> and contains 3.0 mol of an ideal gas. Cylinder Y has a volume&nbsp;<span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"\\frac{V}{2}\"> <mfrac> <mi>V</mi> <mn>2</mn> </mfrac> </math></span> and contains 2.0 mol of the same gas.<br></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">The gases in X and Y are at the same temperature <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"T\"> <mi>T</mi> </math></span><em>.</em> The containers are joined by a valve which is opened so that the temperatures do not change.</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">What is the change in pressure in X?</span></p><p>A. <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\" + \\frac{1}{3}\\left( {\\frac{{RT}}{V}} \\right)\"> <mo>+</mo> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>V</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> <mo>)</mo> </mrow> </math></span></span></p><p>B. <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\" - \\frac{1}{3}\\left( {\\frac{{RT}}{V}} \\right)\"> <mo>−</mo> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>V</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> <mo>)</mo> </mrow> </math></span></span></p><p>C. <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\" + \\frac{2}{3}\\left( {\\frac{{RT}}{V}} \\right)\"> <mo>+</mo> <mfrac> <mn>2</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>V</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> <mo>)</mo> </mrow> </math></span></span></p><p>D. <span class=\"mjpage\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\" - \\frac{2}{3}\\left( {\\frac{{RT}}{V}} \\right)\"> <mo>−</mo> <mfrac> <mn>2</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>V</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> <mo>)</mo> </mrow> </math></span></span></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"19N.1A.SL.TZ0.9","key":"qcm_cd020f734c07","tag":"","session":"19N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":9,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"D","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Under which conditions of pressure and density will a real gas approximate to an ideal gas?</span></p><p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Propositions A à D\"/></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"19N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","key":"qcm_d2e9dc6b08a2","tag":"","session":"19N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"D","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">An ideal gas is in a closed container. Which changes to its volume and temperature when taken together must cause a decrease in the gas pressure?</span></p><p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Propositions A à D\"/></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"19N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","key":"qcm_03ed44b3ee9f","tag":"","session":"19N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Two flasks P and Q contain an ideal gas and are connected with a tube of negligible volume compared to that of the flasks. The volume of P is twice the volume of Q.</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><img style=\"margin-right:auto;margin-left:auto;display:block;\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"257\" height=\"111\"></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">P is held at a temperature of 200 K and Q is held at a temperature of 400 K.</span></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">What is mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>mass</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>gas</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>mass</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>gas</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>?</span></span></p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math></span></span></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></span></span></p><p>C. 4</span></span></p><p>D. 8</span></span></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"20N.1A.SL.TZ0.7","key":"qcm_67d8098ac154","tag":"","session":"20N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":7,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>What is <strong>not</strong> an assumption of the kinetic model of an ideal gas?</p><p>A. Attractive forces between molecules are negligible.</p><p>B. Collision duration is negligible compared with time between collisions.</p><p>C. Molecules suffer negligible momentum change during wall collisions.</p><p>D. Molecular volume is negligible compared with gas volume.</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>C</p> <p>Even though both difficulty and discrimination index are acceptable a significant number of candidates chose incorrect options B or D. The examiners appreciated that this was a challenging question which required some thought partly because it asked what is not an assumption. Candidates need to be aware that although questions are normally phased in a positive sense there will occasionally be ones like this and they need to hold the idea of 'not' when looking at the possible answers. A useful strategy is to look for correct assumptions and when those are identified there should be just one left — the required answer.</p>"},{"id":"20N.1A.SL.TZ0.9","key":"qcm_900660dc4083","tag":"","session":"20N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":9,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p>Two containers X and Y are maintained at the same temperature. X has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> and Y has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>. They both hold an ideal gas. The pressure in X is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math> and the pressure in Y is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math>. The containers are then joined by a tube of negligible volume. What is the final pressure in the containers?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>150</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Pa</mi></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"20N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","key":"qcm_506630153200","tag":"","session":"20N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>An ideal gas of constant mass is heated in a container of constant volume.</p> <p>What is the reason for the increase in pressure of the gas?</p><p>A. The average number of molecules per unit volume increases.</p><p>B. The average force per impact at the container wall increases.</p><p>C. Molecules collide with each other more frequently.</p><p>D. Molecules occupy a greater fractional volume of the container.</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>B</p> <p>Many candidates chose option C which is a typical misconception that collision between molecules has something to do with pressure.</p>"},{"id":"20N.1A.SL.TZ0.12","key":"qcm_8d8b0b07c0fb","tag":"","session":"20N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>A substance in the gas state has a density about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn></math> times less than when it is in the liquid state. The diameter of a molecule is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>. What is the best estimate of the average distance between molecules in the gas state?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>d</mi></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mi>d</mi></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn><mi>d</mi></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>B</p> <p>This question gives good discrimination at HL but less so at SL. Teacher comments felt that the question&nbsp;was too mathematical but it can be noted that it asks for an estimation of the average distance which is&nbsp;related to the cube root of the volume and 1000 is 103. At both levels option D proved a popular&nbsp;alternative suggesting that candidates were forgetting the cube root.</p>"},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ1.10","key":"qcm_f4ff156f3991","tag":"variante 1","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p>Which aspect of thermal physics is best explained by the molecular kinetic model?</p><p>A. The equation of state of ideal gases</p><p>B. The difference between Celsius and Kelvin temperature</p><p>C. The value of the Avogadro constant</p><p>D. The existence of gaseous isotopes</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ1.10","key":"qcm_d6f55b33e326","tag":"variante 2","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>Two ideal gases X and Y are at the same temperature. The mass of a particle of gas X is&nbsp;larger than the mass of a particle of gas Y. Which is correct about the average kinetic energy&nbsp;and the average speed of the particles in gases X and Y?</p><p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Propositions A à D\"/></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ1.12","key":"qcm_3282e5b185de","tag":"","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"D","q":"<p>A quantity of 2.00 mol of an ideal gas is maintained at a temperature of 127 ºC in a container&nbsp;of volume 0.083 m<sup>3</sup>. What is the pressure of the gas?</p><p>A. 8 kPa</p><p>B. 25 kPa</p><p>C. 40 kPa</p><p>D. 80 kPa</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ1.25","key":"qcm_31d04b647dc8","tag":"","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":25,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>What is the relation between the value of the unified atomic mass unit in grams and the value&nbsp;of Avogadro’s constant in mol<sup>−1</sup>?</p><p>A. Their ratio is 1.</p><p>B. Their product is 1.</p><p>C. Their sum is 1.</p><p>D. Their difference is 0.</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","key":"qcm_2f35b9008823","tag":"","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>A sample of oxygen gas with a volume of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math> is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math>. The gas is heated so that it&nbsp;expands at a constant pressure to a final volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>. What is the final temperature&nbsp;of the gas?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>750</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>470</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>370</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","key":"qcm_d6ffae117655","tag":"","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>Two identical containers X and Y each contain an ideal gas. X has <em>N</em> molecules of gas at an&nbsp;absolute temperature of <em>T</em> and Y has 3<em>N</em> molecules of gas at an absolute temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;What is the ratio of the pressures&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mfrac></math>?</p> <p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","key":"qcm_a2c514dfb596","tag":"","session":"21N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"D","q":"<p>The molar mass of an ideal gas is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math>. A fixed mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> of the gas expands at a constant pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>. The graph shows the variation with temperature <em>T</em> of the gas volume <em>V</em>.</p> <p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p> <p>What is the gradient of the graph?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"21N.1A.SL.TZ0.12","key":"qcm_32d5e7548033","tag":"","session":"21N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>A fixed mass of an ideal gas has a volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>, a pressure of <em>p</em> and a temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math>. The gas is compressed to the volume of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>V</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math> and its pressure increases to 12<em>p</em>. What is the new temperature of the gas?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>333</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>606</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"22M.1A.SL.TZ1.12","key":"qcm_db579e54282b","tag":"","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>An ideal gas is maintained at a temperature of 100 K. The variation of the pressure <em>P</em> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mtext>volume</mtext></mfrac></math> of the gas is shown.</p> <p style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p> <p>What is the quantity of the gas?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>mol</mtext></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>200</mn><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>mol</mtext></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>80</mn><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>mol</mtext></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>mol</mtext></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>C</p> <p>This question tested candidate understanding of the relationship between the slope of a graph and the ideal gas law. SL candidates found this question more difficult than their HL counterparts, but in both groups of students, option C was the most frequent (and correct) answer.</p>"},{"id":"22M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","key":"qcm_8cdad079475c","tag":"","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>A quantity of an ideal gas is at a temperature <em>T</em> in a cylinder with a movable piston that traps a length <em>L</em> of the gas. The piston is moved so that the length of the trapped gas is reduced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>L</mi></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math> and the pressure of the gas doubles.</p> <p><img style=\"display:block;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p> <p>What is the temperature of the gas at the end of the change?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mi>T</mi></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mi>T</mi></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>T</mi></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mi>T</mi></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":"<p>C</p> <p>Some comments queried that the Laws of Thermodynamics are not on the syllabus. This question was set as a test of Thermal Physics, topic 3, with option A coming from Mechanics, topic 2, not Thermodynamics.</p>"},{"id":"22M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","key":"qcm_743b57e493c6","tag":"","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p>What is true for an ideal gas?</p><p>A. <em>nRT = Nk</em><sub>B</sub><em>T</em></p><p>B. <em>nRT = k</em><sub>B</sub><em>T</em></p><p>C. <em>RT = Nk</em><sub>B</sub><em>T</em></p><p>D. <em>RT = k</em><sub>B</sub><em>T</em></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"22M.1A.SL.TZ2.12","key":"qcm_3ab501fdd43f","tag":"","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>Which assumption is part of the molecular kinetic model of ideal gases?&nbsp;</p><p>A. The work done on a system equals the change in kinetic energy of the system.</p><p>B. The volume of a gas results from adding the volume of the individual molecules.</p><p>C. A gas is made up of tiny identical particles in constant random motion.</p><p>D. All particles in a gas have kinetic and potential energy.</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"22N.1A.SL.TZ0.10","key":"qcm_920155f589aa","tag":"","session":"22N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p>Three statements about Boltzmann’s constant <em>k</em><sub>B</sub> are:</p> <p style=\"padding-left:90px;\">I.&nbsp; &nbsp;<em>k</em><sub>B</sub> has a&nbsp;unit of J K<sup>−1</sup></p> <p style=\"padding-left:90px;\">II.&nbsp; <em>k</em><sub>B </sub><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>gas&nbsp;constant</mtext><mtext>Avogadro's&nbsp;constant</mtext></mfrac></math></p> <p style=\"padding-left:90px;\">III.&nbsp;<em>k</em><sub>B </sub><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>the&nbsp;average&nbsp;kinetic&nbsp;energy&nbsp;of&nbsp;particles</mtext><mtext>temperature&nbsp;of&nbsp;the&nbsp;gas</mtext></mfrac></math></p> <p>Which statements are correct?</p><p>A. I and II only</p><p>B. I and III only</p><p>C. II and III only</p><p>D. I, II and III</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"22N.1A.SL.TZ0.11","key":"qcm_9b75ec078f87","tag":"","session":"22N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"D","q":"<p>Gases in the atmosphere are compounds of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mtext>C</mtext><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>6</mn><mn>12</mn></mmultiscripts></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mtext>H</mtext><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></mmultiscripts></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mtext>O</mtext><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>8</mn><mn>16</mn></mmultiscripts></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mtext>N</mtext><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>7</mn><mn>14</mn></mmultiscripts></math>.</p> <p>Four of these gases are CO<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O, CH<sub>4</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O. A pure sample of each gas is produced. Each sample has the same mass.</p> <p>Which sample contains the greatest number of molecules?</p><p>A. N<sub>2</sub>O</p><p>B. H<sub>2</sub>O</p><p>C. CO<sub>2</sub></p><p>D. CH<sub>4</sub></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"23M.1A.SL.TZ2.9","key":"qcm_d8f4f5f93e2a","tag":"","session":"23M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":9,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"A","q":"<p>A fixed mass of an ideal gas expands slowly at constant temperature in a container.</p> <p>Three statements about the gas molecules during the expansion are:</p> <p style=\"padding-left:30px;\">I.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; They collide with the walls of the container at a reduced rate.<br>II. &nbsp; They travel further on average between each collision.<br>III.&nbsp; Their average kinetic energy decreases as the gas expands.</p> <p>Which statements are correct?</p><p>A. I and II only</p><p>B. I and III only</p><p>C. II and III only</p><p>D. I, II and III</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"23M.1A.SL.TZ2.10","key":"qcm_929ab9a6b884","tag":"","session":"23M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>A balloon of volume <em>V</em> contains 10 mg of an ideal gas at a pressure <em>P</em>. An additional mass of the gas is added without changing the temperature of the balloon. This change causes the volume to increase to 2<em>V</em> and the pressure to increase to 3<em>P</em>.</p> <p>What is the mass of gas <strong>added</strong> to the balloon?</p><p>A. 5 mg</p><p>B. 15 mg</p><p>C. 50 mg</p><p>D. 60 mg</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"23M.1A.SL.TZ2.11","key":"qcm_47b90f2056f3","tag":"","session":"23M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":11,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>A vessel contains a mass <em>X </em>of helium gas and a mass 2<em>X</em> of oxygen gas.</p> <p style=\"text-align:center;\">Molar mass of helium = 4 g</p> <p style=\"text-align:center;\">Molar mass of oxygen = 32 g</p> <p>What is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>number</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>helium</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>atoms</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>number</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>oxygen</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>molecules</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>C. 4</p><p>D. 8</p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.9","key":"qcm_49d6f9498e08","tag":"","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":9,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>Two samples of a gas are kept in separate containers. The molecules of each sample have the same average translational speed, but the samples have a different density.</p> <p>What is correct about the pressure and the temperature of the samples, as compared to each other?</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Pressure</th><th>Temperature</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>same</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>same</td><td>different</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>different</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>different</td><td>different</td></tr></table><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.10","key":"qcm_3d3b625d3c33","tag":"","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"B","q":"<p>A sample of a gas has volume <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math></em> and contains <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math></em> molecules, each of mass <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math></em>. The average translational speed of the molecules is <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi></math></em>.</p> <p>Which expression is equivalent to the pressure of the gas?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>V</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"EXE.1A.SL.TZ0.12","key":"qcm_745211fe354a","tag":"","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":12,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>The density of an ideal gas is 1.4 kg m<sup>−3</sup> when its pressure is 0.1 MPa.</p> <p>What is the average translational speed of the gas molecules?</p><p>A. 0.46 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>B. 270 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>C. 460 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>D. 71 km s<sup>−1</sup></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""},{"id":"SPM.1A.HL.TZ0.17","key":"qcm_ed69263d9680","tag":"SL + HL","session":"SPM","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":17,"topics":[],"subtopics":[],"answer":"C","q":"<p>Two containers, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math>, are filled with an ideal gas at the same pressure.</p> <p>The volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> is four times the volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math>. The temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> is 327 °C and the temperature&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math> is 27 °C.</p> <p>What is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>amount</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>substance</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>amount</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>substance</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> ?</p><p>A. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>B. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>C. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>D. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math></p><div class=\"qc_marks_available\"><p>[1]</p></div>","ms":"","er":""}],"p2":[{"id":"19M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"19M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","c","di","dii"],"marks":8,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p><span style=\"background-color: #ffffff;\">A container of volume 3.2 × 10-6 m<sup>3</sup> is filled with helium gas at a pressure of 5.1 × 10<sup>5</sup> Pa and temperature 320 K. Assume that this sample of helium gas behaves as an ideal gas.</span></p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p><span style=\"background-color: #ffffff;\">A helium atom has a volume of 4.9 × 10<sup>-31</sup> m<sup>3</sup>.</span></p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">The molar mass of helium is 4.0 g mol<sup>-1</sup>. Show that the mass of a helium atom is 6.6 × 10<sup>-27</sup> kg.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"m = \\frac{{4.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>m</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4.0</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>3</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>6.02</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span>«kg»</span><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"></span></em></p> <p><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><strong>OR</strong></span></em></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">6.64 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">×</span> 10<span style=\"font-size:14px;\"><sup><span style=\"text-align:left;color:#000000;text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;display:inline !important;white-space:normal;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;\">−27 </span></sup></span></span><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«kg»</span><em><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"> ✔</span></em></p> <p> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>The mark was awarded for a clear substitution or an answer to at least 3sf. Many gained the mark for a clear substitution with a conversion from g to kg somewhere in their response. Fewer gave the answer to the correct number of sf.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Estimate the average speed of the helium atoms in the container.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{3}{2}kT/v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3kT}}{m}} /\\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 320}}{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}} \" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mfrac> <mi>m</mi> <mrow> <msup> <mi>v</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </mfrac> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mi>v</mi> <mo>=</mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> <mi>m</mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mn>1.38</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>6.6</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>27</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </msqrt> </math></span> <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">✔</span></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em>v</em> = 1.4 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">×</span> 10<sup>3</sup> «<span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">ms</span><sup><span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">−</span><span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">1</span></sup>» ✔</span> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>At HL this was very well answered but at SL many just worked out E=3/2kT and left it as a value for KE.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Show that the number of helium atoms in the container is about 4 × 10<sup>20</sup>.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"N = \\frac{{pV}}{{kT}}/\\frac{{5.1 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 320}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>N</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mi>V</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi>k</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>5.1</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mn>5</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>1.38</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span></span></p> <p><em><strong>OR</strong></em></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"N = \\frac{{pV{N_A}}}{{RT}}/\\frac{{5.1 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{8.31 \\times 320}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>N</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mi>V</mi> <mrow> <msub> <mi>N</mi> <mi>A</mi> </msub> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mi>R</mi> <mi>T</mi> </mrow> </mfrac> <mrow> <mo>/</mo> </mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>5.1</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mn>5</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>6.02</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>23</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>8.31</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mn>320</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span> <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">✔</span></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em>N</em> = 3.7 <span style=\"display:inline !important;float:none;background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:Verdana , Arial , Helvetica , sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;text-indent:0px;white-space:normal;\">× </span>10<sup>20</sup> ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>Again at HL this was very well answered with the most common approach being to calculate the number of moles and then multiply by N<sub>A</sub> to calculate the number of atoms. At SL many candidates calculated n but stopped there. Also at SL there was some evidence of candidates working backwards and magically producing a value for ‘n’ that gave a result very close to that required after multiplying by N<sub>A</sub>.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(i)","key":"di","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Calculate the ratio <span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{{{\\text{total volume of helium atoms}}}}{{{\\text{volume of helium gas}}}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>total volume of helium atoms</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mrow> <mtext>volume of helium gas</mtext> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> </math></span>.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«<span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><span class=\"mjpage\"><math alttext=\"\\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{20}} \\times 4.9 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}}{{3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} =» 6 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>20</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mo>×</mo> <mn>4.9</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>31</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>3.2</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mn>10</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>6</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mrow> <mo>»</mo> </mrow> <mn>6</mn> <mo>×</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mn>10</mn> <mrow> <mo>−</mo> <mn>5</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math></span> ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>This was well answered with the most common mistake being to use the volume of a single atom rather than the total volume of the atoms.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">di.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(ii)","key":"dii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">Explain, using your answer to (d)(i) and with reference to the kinetic model, why this sample of helium can be assumed to be an ideal gas.</span></p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» the size of the particles is small compared to the distance between them/size of the container/gas</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em><strong>OR</strong></em></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» the volume of the particles is negligible/the volume of the particles is small compared to the volume of the container/gas</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\"><em><strong>OR</strong></em></span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">«For an ideal gas» particles are assumed to be point objects ✔</span></p> <p><span style=\"background-color:#ffffff;\">calculation/ratio/result in (d)(i) shows that volume of helium atoms is negligible compared to/much smaller than volume of helium gas/container «hence assumption is justified» ✔</span></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>In general this was poorly answered at SL. Many other non-related gas properties given such as no / negligible intermolecular forces, low pressure, high temperature. Some candidates interpreted the ratio as meaning it is a low density gas. At HL candidates seemed more able to focus on the key part feature of the question, which was the nature of the volumes involved. Examiners were looking for an assumption of the kinetic theory related to the volume of the atoms/gas and then a link to the ratio calculated in ci). The command terms were slightly different at SL and HL, giving slightly more guidance at SL.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">dii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"21M.2.SL.TZ1.3","session":"21M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ1","num":3,"hits":["ai","aii"],"marks":12,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p>A mass of 1.0 kg of water is brought to its boiling point of 100 °C using an electric heater of power 1.6 kW.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>A mass of 0.86 kg of water remains after it has boiled for 200 s.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>The electric heater has two identical resistors connected in parallel.</p> <p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"/></p> <p>The circuit transfers 1.6 kW when switch A only is closed. The external voltage is 220 V.</p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>The molar mass of water is 18 g mol<sup>−1</sup>. Estimate the average speed of the water molecules in the vapor produced. Assume the vapor behaves as an ideal gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><em>E</em><sub>k</sub> = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>38</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>373</mn><mo>)</mo></math>» = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></msup></math> «J» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>v = </em>«<em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>38</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>373</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>018</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math></em>»<em> = </em>720 «m s<sup>−1</sup>» <strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>State <strong>one</strong> assumption of the kinetic model of an ideal gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>particles can be considered points «without dimensions» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>no intermolecular forces/no forces between particles «except during collisions»<strong>✓</strong></p> <p>the volume of a particle is negligible compared to volume of gas <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>collisions between particles are elastic <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>time between particle collisions are greater than time of collision <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>no intermolecular PE/no PE between particles <strong>✓</strong></p> <p> </p> <p><em>Accept reference to atoms/molecules for “particle”</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.ii.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Estimate the specific latent heat of vaporization of water. State an appropriate unit for your answer.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«<em>mL = P</em> t» so «<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1600</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>» = 2.3 x 10<sup>6</sup> «J kg<sup>-1</sup>» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>J kg<sup>−1 </sup><strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Explain why the temperature of water remains at 100 °C during this time.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>«all» of the energy added is used to increase the «intermolecular» potential energy of the particles/break «intermolecular» bonds/<strong>OWTTE</strong> <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Accept reference to atoms/molecules for “particle”</em> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>The heater is removed and a mass of 0.30 kg of pasta at −10 °C is added to the boiling water.</p> <p>Determine the equilibrium temperature of the pasta and water after the pasta is added. Other heat transfers are negligible.</p> <p style=\"padding-left:180px;\">Specific heat capacity of pasta = 1.8 kJ kg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup><br/>Specific heat capacity of water = 4.2 kJ kg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup></p> <div class=\"marks\">[3]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>use of mcΔT <strong>✓</strong></p> <p>0.86 × 4200 × (100 – <em>T</em>) = 0.3 × 1800 × (<em>T</em> +10) <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>T</em><sub>eq</sub> = 85.69«°C» ≅ 86«°C» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Accept T<sub>eq</sub> in Kelvin (359 K).</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">c.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(i)","key":"di","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Show that each resistor has a resistance of about 30 Ω.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[1]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>so</mi><mo> </mo><mfrac><msup><mn>220</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1600</mn></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>so</mi><mo> </mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></math> «Ω» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>Must see either the substituted values <strong>OR</strong> a value for R to at least three s.f.</em></p> <p> </p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(d)(ii)","key":"dii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Calculate the power transferred by the heater when both switches are closed.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>use of parallel resistors addition so <em>R</em><sub>eq</sub> = 15 «Ω» <strong>✓</strong></p> <p><em>P</em> = 3200 «W» <strong>✓</strong></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">d.ii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"22M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"22M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","bi","bii"],"marks":6,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":[],"pre":"<div class=\"specification\"> <p>A fixed mass of an ideal gas is contained in a cylinder closed with a frictionless piston. The volume of the gas is 2.5 × 10<sup>−3 </sup>m<sup>3</sup> when the temperature of the gas is 37 °C and the pressure of the gas is 4.0 × 10<sup>5 </sup>Pa.</p> </div><div class=\"specification\"> <p>Energy is now supplied to the gas and the piston moves to allow the gas to expand. The temperature is held constant.</p> </div>","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Calculate the number of gas particles in the cylinder.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>Correct conversion of T «T = 310 K» seen ✓</p> <p>« use of = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> to get » 2.3 × 10<sup>23</sup> ✓</p> <p> </p> <p><em>Allow <strong>ECF</strong> from <strong>MP1</strong> i.e., T in Celsius (Result is 2.7 x 10<sup>24</sup>)</em></p> <p><em>Allow use of n, R and N<sub>A</sub></em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>a) This was well answered with the majority converting to K. Quite a few found the number of moles but did not then convert to molecules.</p> <p>bi) Well answered. It was pleasing to see how many recognised the need to state that the mass/number of molecules stayed the same as well as stating that the volume increased. At SL this recognition was less common so only 1 mark was often awarded.</p> <p>bii) This was less successfully answered. A surprising number of candidates said that the internal energy of an ideal gas increases during an isothermal expansion. Many recognised that constant temp meant constant KE but then went on to state that the PE must increase and so the internal energy would increase.</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">a.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Discuss, for this process, the changes that occur in the density of the gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>density decreases ✓</p> <p>volume is increased <em><strong>AND</strong> </em>mass/number of particles remains constant ✓</p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.i.</div> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;\"> <p>Discuss, for this process, the changes that occur in the internal energy of the gas.</p> <div class=\"marks\">[2]</div> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> <p>internal energy is constant ✓</p> <p>internal energy depends on kinetic energy/temperature «only»</p> <p><em><strong>OR</strong></em></p> <p>since temperature/kinetic energy is constant ✓</p> <p> </p> <p><em>Do not award <strong>MP2</strong> for stating that “temperature is constant” unless linked to the correct conclusion, as that is mentioned in the stem.</em></p> <p><em>Award <strong>MP2</strong> for stating that kinetic energy remains constant.</em></p> <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>","er":"<div class=\"question\" style=\"padding-left: 20px;\"> [N/A] <div class=\"question_part_label\">b.ii.</div> </div>"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"22N.2.SL.TZ0.2","session":"22N","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":2,"hits":["bi","bii"],"marks":9,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (a.i) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the minimum area of the solar heating panel required to increase the temperature of all the water in the tank to 30°C during a time of 1.0 hour. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> energy required = 250 × 4200 × (30 − 15) ✓ </p> <p> energy available = 0.30 × 680 × <em> t </em> × <em> A </em> ✓ </p> <p> <em> A </em> = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 250 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 4200 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 15 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 30 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 680 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 21 «m <sup> 2 </sup> » <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> 22 «m <sup> 2 </sup> » ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> ECF </strong> from <strong> MP1 </strong> and <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> <p> <em> Accept the correct use of 0.30 in either <strong> MP1 </strong> or <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Most candidates had a good attempt at this but there were often slight slips. Some missed the efficiency of the process. Some included the albedo of the roof tiles. Some thought that the temperature rise needed to have 273 added to convert to kelvin. However, sometimes scoring through ECF (error carried forward), the average mark was around 2 marks. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (a.ii) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Estimate, in °C, the temperature of the roof tiles. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> absorbed intensity = (1 − 0.2) × 680 «= 544» «W m <sup> −2 </sup> » </p> <p> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> </p> <p> emitted intensity = 0.97 × 5.67 × 10 <sup> −8 </sup> × T <sup> 4 </sup> ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> T </em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mroot> <mfrac> <mn> 544 </mn> <mrow> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 97 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 67 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> <mn> 4 </mn> </mroot> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 315 </mn> </math> «K» ✓ </p> <p> 42 «°C» ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> ECF </strong> from <strong> MP1 </strong> and <strong> MP2 </strong> . </em> </p> <p> <em> Allow <strong> MP1 </strong> if absorbed or emitted intensity is multiplied by area. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> This was a bit more hit and miss than the previous question part. One common mistake was not understanding what albedo meant. Some took it as the amount of energy absorbed rather than reflected. Emissivity was often missed. Several candidates, successfully answering the question or not, were able to score MP3 converting the final temperature into Celsius degrees. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (b.i) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State <strong> one </strong> way in which a real gas differs from an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> can be liquefied ✓ </p> <p> has intermolecular forces / potential energy ✓ </p> <p> has atoms/molecules that are not point objects / take up volume ✓ </p> <p> does not follow the ideal gas law «for all <em> T </em> and <em> p </em> » ✓ </p> <p> collisions between particles are non-elastic ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Accept the converse argument. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> This was very well answered. Candidates showed an understanding of the differences between ideal and real gases. </p> </div>"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":[],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qn_code_number\"> (b.ii) </div> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The water is heated. Explain why the quantity of air in the storage tank decreases. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 1 </strong> </em> </p> <p> «constant <em> p </em> and <em> V </em> imply» <em> nT </em> = const ✓ </p> <p> <em> T </em> increases hence <em> n </em> decreases ✓ </p> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 2 </strong> </em> </p> <p> «constant <em> p </em> and <em> n </em> imply» <em> V </em> is proportional to <em> T </em> / air expands as it is heated ✓ </p> <p> «original» air occupies a greater volume <strong> OR </strong> some air leaves through opening ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> <strong> MP2 </strong> in <strong> ALT 2 </strong> must come from expansion of air, not from expansion of water. </em> </p> <p> <em> Award <strong> [0] </strong> for an answer based on expansion of water. </em> </p> <p> <em> Award <strong> [1] </strong> <strong> max </strong> for an answer based on convection currents. </em> </p> </div>","er":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> It was surprising to see a large number of answers based on the expansion of water, as the stem of the question clearly states that the level of water remains constant. Most successful candidates scored by quoting <em> pV </em> constant so concluding with the inverse relationship of n and <em> T </em> , others also managed to score by explaining that the volume of air increases and therefore must go out through the opening. Answers based on convection currents were given partial credit. </p> </div>"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"23M.2.SL.TZ2.2","session":"23M","level":"SL","tz":"TZ2","num":2,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3","T.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State the unit for <em> pV </em> in fundamental SI units. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> kg m <sup> 2 </sup> s <sup> −2 </sup> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3","T.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine, using the graph, whether the gas acts as an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 1 </strong> </em> </p> <p> Graph shown is a straight line/linear <br/> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <br/> expected graph should be a straight line/linear ✓ </p> <p> If ideal then <em> T </em> intercept must be at <em> T </em> = −273 °C ✓ </p> <p> Use of <em> y </em> = <em> mx </em> + <em> c </em> to show that <em> x </em> = −273 °C when <em> y </em> = 0 ✓ <br/> (hence ideal) </p> <p> <em> <strong> ALTERNATIVE 2 </strong> </em> </p> <p> Calculates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> </math> for two different points ✓ <br/> Obtains 1.50 «J K <sup> −1 </sup> » for both ✓ </p> <p> States that for ideal gas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <mi> n </mi> <mi> R </mi> </math> which is constant and concludes that gas is ideal ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate, in g, the mass of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Use of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> n </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi> R </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> N </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mi> k </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Mass of gas = <em> n </em> × <em> N </em> <sub> A </sub> × mass of molecule <br/> <em> <strong> OR </strong> </em> <br/> Mass of gas = N <em> </em> × mass of molecule ✓ </p> <p> 5.1 «g» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"EXE.2.HL.TZ0.1","session":"EXE","level":"HL","tz":"TZ0","num":1,"hits":["cii"],"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State the principal energy change in nuclear fission. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Mass-energy «of uranium» into kinetic energy of fission products ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The energy released in the reaction is about 180 MeV. Estimate, in J, the energy released when 1 kg of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> U </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 92 </mn> <mn> 235 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> undergoes fission. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Mass of uranium nucleus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> ≈ </mo> <mn> 235 </mn> <mtext> u </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mtext> energy </mtext> <mtext> mass </mtext> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 180 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 6 </mn> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 19 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 235 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 661 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 27 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 7 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 13 </mn> </msup> </math> «J» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(i)","key":"bi","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State and explain the decay mode of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> beta minus decay ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> has more neutrons / higher <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mi> N </mi> <mi> Z </mi> </mfrac> </math> ratio than stable nuclides of similar <em> A </em> «and beta minus reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mi> N </mi> <mi> Z </mi> </mfrac> </math> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(ii)","key":"bii","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate, in s <sup> −1 </sup> , the initial activity of the sample. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> e </mi> <mn> 25 </mn> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 7 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 10 </mn> </msup> </math> «s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(iii)","key":"biii","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Show that the decay constant of a nuclide is given by − <em> m </em> , where <em> m </em> is the slope of the graph of ln <em> A </em> against <em> t </em> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Takes ln of both sides of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> A </mi> <mo> = </mo> <msub> <mi> A </mi> <mn> 0 </mn> </msub> <msup> <mi> e </mi> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </msup> </math> , leading to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <mi> A </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <msub> <mi> A </mi> <mn> 0 </mn> </msub> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> <mi> t </mi> </math> ✓ </p> <p> «hence slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mo> - </mo> <mi> λ </mi> </math> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)(iv)","key":"biv","subtopics":["E.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine, in days, the half-life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mmultiscripts> <mtext> Te </mtext> <mprescripts> </mprescripts> <mn> 52 </mn> <mn> 132 </mn> </mmultiscripts> </math> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Slope =«−» <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </math> «s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> T </mi> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </mfrac> </msub> <mo> = </mo> </math> « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> ln </mi> <mo> </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 5 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 60 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 24 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 3.2 «days» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(i)","key":"ci","subtopics":["E.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline the role of the heat exchanger in a nuclear power station. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Collects thermal energy from the coolant and delivers it to the gas ✓ </p> <p> Prevents the «irradiated» coolant from leaving the reactor vessel ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(ii)","key":"cii","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the maximum temperature of the gas during the cycle. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Correct read offs of pressure and volume at B ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 6 </mn> </msup> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 3 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 31 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <mi> T </mi> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> T </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 1500 </mn> </math> «K» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(iii)","key":"ciii","subtopics":["B.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline why the entropy of the gas remains constant during changes BC and DA. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> S </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> Q </mi> </mrow> <mi> T </mi> </mfrac> </math> , the change in entropy is zero when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> Q </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)(iv)","key":"civ","subtopics":["B.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the efficiency of the cycle. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Net work done = « <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 23 </mn> <mo> + </mo> <mn> 9 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 11 </mn> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 32 </mn> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 3 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 25 </mn> </math> » 9.77 «kJ» ✓ </p> <p> Efficiency =« <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 9 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 77 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 20 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 58 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> </math> » 0.47 ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(d)","key":"d","subtopics":["A.4"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Show that the rotational kinetic energy of the turbine decreases at a constant rate. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Rotational KE is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> ω </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Calculation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msup> <mi> ω </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> for at least four points, e.g. {96.1, 76.7, 57.6, 38.4, 19.3}×10 <sup> 3 </sup> </p> <p> Shows that the differences in equal time intervals are approximately the same, e.g. {19.4, 19.1, 19.2, 19.1, 19.3}×10 <sup> 3 </sup> ✓ </p> <p> </p> <p> <em> Allow a tolerance of ±1×10 <sup> 3 </sup> s <sup> −2 </sup> from the values stated in MP2. </em> </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.7","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":7,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> State <strong> two </strong> assumptions of the kinetic model of an ideal gas that refer to intermolecular collisions. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> The collisions are elastic ✓ </p> <p> The time for a collision is much shorter than the time between collisions ✓ </p> <p> The intermolecular forces are only present during collisions ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Discuss how the motion of the molecules of a gas gives rise to pressure in the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> The momentum of a molecule changes when it collides with a container wall ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> F </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> and Newton’s third law, this leads to a force exerted on the wall by the molecule ✓ </p> <p> The average force exerted by all the molecules on a unit area of the wall is equivalent to pressure ✓ </p> <p> </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The average speed of the molecules of a gas is 500 m s <sup> −1 </sup> . The density of the gas is 1.2 kg m <sup> −3 </sup> . Calculate, in kPa, the pressure of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 500 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> 100 «kPa» ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.8","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":8,"hits":["a","b","c"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of air molecules. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 1 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 8 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mn> 5 </mn> </msup> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 520 </mn> </math> «m s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen and other gases. Explain why the component gases of air in the container have different average translational speeds. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [3] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Average kinetic energy of the molecules is determined by the temperature only ✓ </p> <p> The mass of a molecule is different for each component gas ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> E </mi> <mtext> k </mtext> </msub> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 2 </mn> </mfrac> <mi> m </mi> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> , the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> E </mi> <mtext> k </mtext> </msub> </math> and different mass implies a different average velocity ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The temperature of the sample is increased without a change in pressure. Outline the effect it has on the density of the gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <strong> <em> ALTERNATIVE 1 </em> </strong> </p> <p> The average translational speed increases «because <em> T </em> increases» ✓ </p> <p> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mn> 1 </mn> <mn> 3 </mn> </mfrac> <mi> ρ </mi> <msup> <mi> v </mi> <mn> 2 </mn> </msup> </math> , the density decreases «to keep <em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> </math> </em> constant» ✓ </p> <p> <br/> <strong> <em> ALTERNATIVE 2 </em> </strong> </p> <p> From the ideal gas law, the volume of the gas increases ✓ </p> <p> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> ρ </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mi> m </mi> <mi> v </mi> </mfrac> </math> and <em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> m </mi> </math> </em> is constant, the density decreases ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.9","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":9,"hits":["ai","aii","b","c"],"marks":8,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Outline how the concept of absolute zero of temperature is interpreted in terms of: </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which the volume </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> the pressure extrapolates to zero (can be shown by sketch) ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(i)","key":"ai","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> the ideal gas law, </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which the volume </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> the pressure extrapolates to zero (can be shown by sketch) ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(a)(ii)","key":"aii","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> the kinetic energy of particles in an ideal gas. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> it is the temperature at which all the (random) motion stops </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> at which all the motion can be extrapolated to stop </p> <p> <strong> <em> OR </em> </strong> </p> <p> at which the kinetic energy of all particles is zero ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> A container holds a mixture of argon and helium atoms at a temperature of 37 °C. </p> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of the argon atoms. </p> <p> The molar mass of argon is 4.0 × 10 <sup> −2 </sup> kg mol <sup> −1 </sup> . </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [4] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> Use of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 0 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> <msub> <mi> N </mi> <mtext> A </mtext> </msub> </mfrac> <mfenced> <mrow> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 26 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kg </mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Work showing that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mfenced> <mrow> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mi> ρ </mi> </mfrac> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> p </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> m </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> </msqrt> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> k </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> <mi> m </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> </math> ✓ </p> <p> Correct substitution AND conversion to K (310 K) ✓ </p> <p> 430/440 «m s <sup> −1 </sup> » ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Discuss how the mean kinetic energy of the argon atoms in the mixture compares with that of the helium atoms. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [2] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> the gases are in the same container at the same temperature so are <span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"> in equilibrium </span> ✓ </p> <p> they must have the same mean/average kinetic energy ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true},{"id":"EXE.2.SL.TZ0.10","session":"EXE","level":"SL","tz":"TZ0","num":10,"hits":["a","b","c","d"],"marks":7,"parts":[{"label":"(a)","key":"a","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the pressure of the gas in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> p </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mi> n </mi> <mi> R </mi> <mi> T </mi> </mrow> <mi> V </mi> </mfrac> </math> seen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 12 </mn> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kPa </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(b)","key":"b","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Determine the mass of the gas in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mn> 6 </mn> <mo> . </mo> <mn> 4 </mn> <mo> × </mo> <msup> <mn> 10 </mn> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 2 </mn> </mrow> </msup> <mo> </mo> <mtext> kg </mtext> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(c)","key":"c","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> Calculate the average translational speed of the gas particles. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [1] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> v </mi> <mo> = </mo> <msqrt> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 3 </mn> <mi> P </mi> <mi> V </mi> </mrow> <mi> M </mi> </mfrac> </msqrt> <mo> = </mo> <mn> 450 </mn> <mo> </mo> <msup> <mtext> m s </mtext> <mrow> <mo> - </mo> <mn> 1 </mn> </mrow> </msup> </math> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"},{"label":"(d)","key":"d","subtopics":["B.3"],"pre":"","q":"<div class=\"t_qn_question_content\"> <div class=\"qc_body js-toggle-question\"> <p> The temperature of the gas in the container is increased. </p> <p> Explain, using the kinetic theory, how this change leads to a change in pressure in the container. </p> </div> <div class=\"qc_marks_available\"> <p> [4] </p> </div> </div>","ms":"<div class=\"card-body\"> <p> increased temperature means increased average KE and hence increased average translational speed ✓ </p> <p> This increases the momentum transfer at the walls for each collision / <em> mv </em> is greater per collision ✓ </p> <p> This increases the frequency of collisions at the walls / particles cover the distance between walls more quickly ✓ </p> <p> Ideas that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <msub> <mi> F </mi> <mtext> wall </mtext> </msub> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> p </mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext> Δ </mtext> <mi> t </mi> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> AND <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi> P </mi> <mo> = </mo> <mfrac> <mi> F </mi> <mi> A </mi> </mfrac> </math> (can be in words) so that force increases and pressure <span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"> increases </span> ✓ </p> </div>","er":"None"}],"aliases":[],"complete":true}]};
